 discovered and that are associated  with disease is increasing so this is uh  this graph is uh taken from information  from hemd the human gene mutation  database and you can see that every year  a larger number of genes are deposited  into this database  and are associated with with disease  now the question is how are these  how are these  genes associated with certain diseases  what is what is the evidence that is  used uh to kind of promote and to kind  of support those gene disease  associations and uh here's the list of  on the left is a list of different  databases  and sources that we use we look at when  trying to determine whether a gene  disease association does exist now  the issue with this is that a lot of  these databases they deposit information  they don't check the veracity or they  don't check the level of evidence of  these gene disease associations so it's  not always  it may be one paper one publication  looking at one family somewhere  that  creates or that  indicates or suggests that a gene  disease association however is it  truly there that that remains to be seen  and it's the job of the genetic  laboratories to look into that to  determine whether these gene disease  associations truly exist or not  so the best way to kind of demonstrate  the issue of the gene disease  associations is with the case that i'm  going to show here so  part of our original 70 gene panel we  included two genes that are shown down  here gjb6 and myo1a and i'll be talking  about these uh in detail  um  however so we'll start with the with  with this case  it's a seven month old male who  presented with congenital profound  sensor neural hearing loss  and when we ran this uh  the sample for this patient on the other  genome  we identified two variants we identified  a frame shift variant in the gjb6 gene  and we also identified a missense  variant in the myo1a gene so the  question was  do we think that either of these  variants are related to this individual  to this  patient's hearing loss or  is one of them positive for the hearing  loss or not and so that's kind of the  the novel their assessment  process that we undergo every time we do  clinical testing  so we'll start with the gjb6 there so  gjb6 variant um is  associates to a very well known uh  hearing loss gene gjb2 it's uh  supposedly co-localizes to the cochlea  to the inner ear and might create some  plays a role in in gap junction  formation  uh we don't have sufficient  evidence to really understand what it  does and what its true role is in in the  inner ear  now it has been associated with several  different diseases based on the  literature and based on om and different  databases and it's one of  one of his disease associations is with  hydraulic ectodermal dysplasia two  miscellaneous have been identified uh  individuals with that disease  um and it seems that there's several  reports that uh support that so we're  pretty uh you know we're pretty uh  pleased with the evidence that  associated with hydraulic ectodermal  dysplasia however keep in mind that our  patient doesn't have hydraulic  ectodermal dysplasia  they presented with the non-syndromic  hearing loss  um  it's also gjp6 has also been associated  with autosomal dominant hearing loss um  there's two different reports that uh  make that suggestion uh in one report  it's a missense variant that has been  associated with autosomal dominant  hearing loss in the other it's a frame  shift variant shown here  and these two uh  however the evidence is just based off  of one family it's one report for each  one of these  and if you look closer at these variants  and use additional data we found that  these variants both are present in the  population so they're found in exact so  it's you know we  don't know if uh if this if this gene is  related to another form of dominant  hearing loss but we don't think that the  information we have based on these two  variants is sufficient to make that  assumption  now what has been established is that  gjb6 deletions large deletions has been  associated with non-syndromic hearing  loss and we've seen this figure before  earlier  we know that deletions that affect gjb6  we know that deletions that  are upstream of the gdb2  gene affect  the  transcription of gjb2 so it's not really  deletions of gjb6 that are causing gear  losses rather than the lesions  uh that lie upstream of gjb2 and affect  its expression which cause hearing loss  so  again  the  the the gene disease association with  gjb6 is not really understood and so  it becomes difficult it becomes  difficult for us to try to identify or  try to classify the variance so in this  case it's a frameshift variant the  evidence suggests that it hasn't been  reported in individuals with hearing  loss this this specific variant has been  seen in the control population although  it's a very low frequency so it's  possible that it's still causative of  something  um and because it's a frame shift we  know that it may cause a loss of  function of the of the protein  and so it will have a detrimental effect  on the protein so it you know we can't  rule it out however because we don't  know if jgb like  these variants that affect ggb6 uh  specifically affect uh cause  non-syndromic hearing loss we can't say  that this very is pathogenic for the  hearing loss as seen in our patient and  so  this there we classified as a variant of  unknown clinical significance  what about the second variant that was  seen in this individual the second  variant was a myo1a variant  miscellaneous in exon 3.  here's what we know about maya 1a in  both omen and engine reviews two very  well known databases  that curate this type of information  both associate maya 1a with  autosomal dominant deafness and you can  see that here om as well as gene reviews  so we looked at that specifically what  drove both of these databases to include  that gene disease association and all of  it is based on one uh publication  um which  found my 1a variants in a large cohort  of hearing loss individuals so we looked  at all the variants that were listed in  that publication  the publication was reported in two thou  was  published in 2003 and since then we've  had the opportunities to sequence a  large number of individuals a large  number of control populations and just  get more information information on  uncommon variants  and what we identified is that a large  number of the variants that were  identified in this patient in this  cohort  were either  found in  found and control populations making it  less likely that these variants are  positive for hearing loss uh in one case  one of the variants was found at one  percent  the majority of the variance that i  classify when i'm looking at hearing  loss  very various identified hearing loss  patients is that anything that's above  one percent i'll buy except for a few  common uh hearing loss variants they're  likely to be benign so anything above  one percent is likely to be denied  for hearing loss another variant was  seen at 3.8 percent of the population  even more so less likely to be causative  for hearing loss uh so a lot of these  variants were seen control population so  we uh you know so that makes this gene  you know the gene disease association  between my 1a and hearing loss less  likely because this is the only piece of  evidence that we have and and now we can  exclude it because these variants have  been seen at such high frequencies this  is not to say that my 1a is not positive  for hearing loss it's just given the  current evidence that we have we need  more information to know whether truly  this gene disease association exists or  not  so how did we classify the missiles  there that we identified in our patient  uh it was not reported in any other  individuals that we've uh  or in large populations uh the  computational prediction programs uh  they look at misunderstandings and and  we look at conservation and a lot of  other elements to try to understand  whether um  whether other  you know whether in this sense change is  actually pathogenic or not they didn't  provide any information to or did not  provide evidence for or against  autogenicity  so we classified this then as a variant  of unknown clinical significance  so in to summarize this case we  identified two variants  both were classified as variants of  unknown significance  and so the the report went out as an  inclusive report  now this in my opinion is  the reason it went out as an inclusive  report is because we identified two  variants and two genes that we did not  really know whether the gene disease  association existed for these genes with  hearing loss um  and it becomes not an issue of  you know the way that we classify the  variance but it becomes an issue of  whether to include these genes on  hearing loss panels we don't know enough  about these genes associated with  hearing loss uh they only add confusion  to the report they add they definitely  take time to interpret and analyze uh  sanger follow-up has to confirm all  these variants that we identify so it  just takes more time more energy more uh  greater cost to be able to go after  these virus when in essence we don't  even know if they're truly associated  with a disease  so what we did was we found that there's  a large number of hearing loss genes  that have been associated based on  evidence that was not strong that was  not compelling um and so what we went  back and did we looked at all the uh all  the genes that are hearing a lot on our  hearing loss panels as well as other  commercial laboratories  who offer hearing loss testing and we  bend the genes based on evidence into  one of these  five categories whether there was an  association  or if there was not a strong or  compelling association and the the way  we did that was we looked at the  variants that were identified in these  reports  that were identified in these genes and  whether those variants were  segregated with a disease  whether there was any functional  analysis or in vitro uh  or in vivo mouse models or animal  studies that were done to knock out the  gene and see the effect of that gene in  animals so we kind of used all that  evidence  that was available to us to kind of  either to either classify the gene as  having a strong association or a weaker  association  and when we did that we looked at 145  different genes that are available in  our panel as well as other panels and we  found that  54 of those genes  that's about 37 percent of all the genes  that we looked at did not have strong  evidence or compelling evidence  to be  called non-syndromic  hearing loss genes so we're not saying  that these 54 genes are not related to  hearing loss we're just saying that the  evidence would lead to inconclusive  reports as i've shown earlier and it  just leads to more complication and  testing and interpretation  so the idea would be to be able to  exclude these genes from future clinical  gene panels so that you're able to uh  provide a better clinical sensitivity  and clearer reports to the patient and  to the  physician  and one way to do that is there's  several is to be to for for clinical  molecular laboratories to share  information on variants as well as genes  and to share their experiences  um in trying to interpret these genes  and try to interpret these variants uh  one such effort that's uh there's  several efforts out there one such  effort is carried out by the clinical  genome resource task force uh it's  headed by it's headed by our  laboratory director here heidi reed  and  essentially the idea is that all  laboratories share the variance and  their classifications and what genes  they've classified as well as you know  the way they're looking at gene disease  associations and what they've included  on their gene panels and this will help  us  grow our  cause not knowledge database so that  we're able to better  include certain genes on on different  hearing panels whether it be hearing  loss or cardiomyopathy or any disease  area but also to  systema systematically classify variants  the same way and use data across labs to  be able to support that classification  so to summarize really quickly when  we're you know i  the our experience with clinical genetic  testing panels uh  led to some challenges both on the  technical end as well as in the  interpretive end  and it's important to really consider  both of these when when when when  approaching  uh maturation sequencing panels will try  to develop new panels when trying to add  new genes that everything is looked at  uh you know and everything is considered  you know in terms of copy number variant  detection as well as pseudogene homology  for the technical side but also you know  really  looking at genes uh and gene disease  associations critically just to assess  whether they should be included on  panels or not  so  with that i'd like to stop here and just  give an opportunity for us to have a  discussion if you have any questions  related to this or related to ngs  clinical gene panels in general  i also want to very quickly thank the uh  the organizers of the event as well as  rachel for for  and don for hosting me and allow me uh  giving me this opportunity to speak to  everyone here today  thank you for that informative  presentation before we get started on  the question answer session i would like  to remind our audience how to submit  questions  you can submit questions by typing them  in the q a box which can be found by  clicking on the green q a button at the  lower left of the presentation window  we'll try to answer as many questions as  we can  and our first question is  what about using a nano string to  confirm cnv results  so uh yes i've heard of uh several  laboratories in the stream i think uh  we have i don't have any experience  personally with it but i think uh any  type of uh secondary assay that has been  validated uh it could be used for for  copy number during detection  okay so this next question it was posed  kind of differently it it reads used to  singer sequencing  will my lab have to go ngs  sequencing  uh well that i mean it all depends on  what  what kind of  testing that you offer so you know to go  to an earlier slide if you are  interested in  um  it really depends on what  kind of testing that you do so if you're  only doing single gene testing if you're  doing a handful of genes then sanger  sequencing still makes sense it still  has a fast turnaround time and it's  relatively cheaper to other  methodologies however if you're  expanding to  70 gene panels as we are doing with  hearing loss if you're expanding to the  exome which many labs are doing now  to be able to capture that is virtually  very cost ineffective and very time  consuming to do that with anger  sequencing so i think it's a  it's it depends on what the strategy of  the testing in your ladders and the  number of genes and the diseases of  course you know again if you're looking  at  a single disease that has a single  variant uh or a single gene that's  associated with disease for example  cystic fibrosis  you can get you can  do fine by just uh sequencing the cf  gene uh however if you want to do a  pulmonary panel which there's more than  50 genes including the cfg and then no  next generation sequencing would be the  way to go  all right so it looks like those were  all of the submitted questions  and we'll go ahead and wrap this up  today's webcast will be available for  on-demand viewing through november 2015.  you will receive an email from  labyrinths alerting you when this  webcast will be available for replay  we invite you to forward that  announcement to your colleagues who may  have missed today's live event  see you next time goodbye
 

